WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.623
- [Woman Off Camera] Oh, whoa, whoa, stop.

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:03.423
(laughing)

00:00:03.423 --> 00:00:04.256
- Say what?

00:00:04.256 --> 00:00:05.623
Let me know when you're recording.

00:00:05.841 --> 00:00:06.948
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.948 --> 00:00:09.448
(tea pouring)

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.390
- Hello audience members.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:13.730
This is Nick Offerman

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.390
and I'm about to be spilling some tea

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:18.793
in your direction, figuratively.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.910
The character of Ron
Swanson was created by

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.003
Mike Schur and Greg Daniels.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:32.340
They met a Burbank city
official who was anti-government

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.480
and a libertarian and they
thought that was really funny.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.420
They didn't know that I
was gonna play Ron Swanson

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:39.930
when they thought of the character.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.987
But then, almost immediately they said,

00:00:41.987 --> 00:00:43.287
"Oh, we like Nick.

00:00:43.287 --> 00:00:45.397
"So, he's gonna have a moustache.

00:00:45.397 --> 00:00:47.003
"He talks very slowly.

00:00:47.077 --> 00:00:51.000
"We should probably justify his
love of red meat in some way

00:00:51.057 --> 00:00:52.507
"because Nick is spilling out

00:00:52.507 --> 00:00:55.617
"over his pleated JC Penney trousers."

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.560
The scripts on Parks and Recreation

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.203
were so good that we
never needed to improvise.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.180
But we had such a cast
of amazing creative heads

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.010
that they would then encourage us

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:11.890
once we had achieved the script

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.713
if we had any good ideas,
go ahead throw 'em in.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.970
When I ordered that platter
that serves 12 people

00:01:19.061 --> 00:01:22.057
and I say, "I know what I'm about son."

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.300
I believe I came up with that line.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.700
But, it was just, they
already had a line there.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.493
It was all very collaborative.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.003
Most of the credit has
to go to the writers.

00:01:37.428 --> 00:01:39.180
It was the episode where everybody

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.020
got to pick their bachelor party

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.864
and the character of Jerry, Jim O'Heir,

00:01:43.864 --> 00:01:46.240
took us to his ice cream shop.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.600
And we were all indulgently
chowing down on ice cream

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.770
and Chris Pratt had a huge
cone of strawberry ice cream

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.763
and he sculpted a vagina into it.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.933
And just kept surreptitiously
showing it to me.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.210
And you know, you try not laughing

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.790
when Chris Pratt shows you

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.753
his tiny strawberry ice cream vagina.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.190
She's my teacher, she's my hero.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:17.630
I saw her on Will and Grace
18 years ago when we met.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.520
It was like seeing Mel
Brooks or Carol Burnett

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.700
or just some legend.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.040
I thought, oh, you were
born an encyclopedia

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.263
of great comedy.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.020
She's 11 years older than me,

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.820
so there's always been a great
teacher student relationship.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:33.650
Parks and Rec was the first time

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.363
when she played Tammy Two,

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:38.300
the first time that we got
to work together as a team.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.580
We'd often worked together,

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.023
but never where it was the two of us.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:43.640
It was incredibly difficult

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.503
because of all the people in the world,

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.440
she knows how to push my buttons.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.420
And so to then put that in a scene

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.670
where she's being funny

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.470
and I'm not supposed to laugh at her

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.520
was an incredibly difficult task.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.803
And sometimes I succeeded.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.070
We all keep in touch, we
have an ongoing cast thread

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.510
that we text on almost daily.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:09.853
Somebody's always got something going on.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.500
A couple times a year we're
trying to get together

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:13.430
to have dinner.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:15.240
Retta has a new show on NBC,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.363
Retta also has a book coming out.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.993
So the thread's been a little Retta heavy,

00:03:21.611 --> 00:03:23.173
but that's great.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.286
Amy just finished directing
a movie called Wine Country.

00:03:26.286 --> 00:03:29.193
Everybody's always got stuff happening.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.363
It's a wonderful family feeling
keeping us all in touch.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.490
The best thing I took from the set

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:43.700
was all of the trim and doors and windows

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.680
from the set of the main
Parks and Rec office.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.080
Somehow found their way to my wood shop

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.610
and we milled them up
into strips of red oak

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.290
and then glued them up into planks

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.333
and made them into canoe paddles,

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:01.963
which we then gifted to
the cast and producers.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:03.750
And hanging in my shop

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:08.060
is that original huge framed picture

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:12.543
of a brunette woman holding
out a plate of bacon and eggs.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.940
It's what I'm referring to when I say,

00:04:15.074 --> 00:04:16.653
"I'm a simple man,

00:04:16.967 --> 00:04:21.477
"I like ladies with dark
hair and breakfast food."

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:26.393
So that hangs in my shop
and inspires me everyday.

